{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Thus you shall say to my lord, to Esav.</b> Yaakov was not calling Esav 'my lord' behind his back. Rather, the meaning is that he instructed them to say to any inquirer among his servants and household members, 'to my lord, to Esav,' as it is written: ״it is a gift sent to my lord, to Esav.״" "<b>With Lavan I sojourned.</b> In this message Yaakov needed to convey three matters. The first: that his separation from his father for twenty-two years was not out of fear and dread, for Esav had not pursued him at all; rather, he sojourned with Lavan under the burden of labor, as it says (Psalms 120:5) ״כי גרתי משך״, and therefore he needed to delay until now. The second: he informed him that he is wealthy, and that the Holy One, blessed be He, had enriched him as the reward of his labor — his wealth would come from the toil of his own hands, not from an inheritance of fathers in which Esav would have a share; and this is what he said: ״and I acquired ox and donkey, flocks,״ etc. The third: he informed him that he seeks his peace and desires his love and asks for his favor — and this is ״and I send it to tell my lord, to find favor in your eyes.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:59.049139223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}